    63	the smallest case. In [1] the shape of the support of possible counterexamples to the Jacobian
    64	Conjecture in dimension 2 is described in great detail. Based on its results, and some results of [2],
    65	in [5] a list of possible corners of small counterexample is given. We will adapt the techniques
    66	of [1], [2] and [3] in order to discard nearly all the cases with max{deg(P ), deg(Q)} < 125, which
    67	haven’t been discarded previously. The only exception is the case (deg P, deg Q) = (72, 108), and
    68	so, if one manages to discard this case, it would increase the lower bound from 108 up to 125.
    69	   The article is organized as follows. In section 2 we list all the cases with max{deg(P ), deg(Q)} <
    70	125, following [5]. There are 10 cases, from which we consider 5 to be discarded already by pre-
    71	vious work of different authors. If we assume deg(P ) < deg(Q), then the remaining 5 cases
    72	satisfy
    73	                       (deg(P ), deg(Q)) ∈ {(56, 84), (66, 99), (72, 108), (80, 120)},
    74	where there are two cases with (deg(P ), deg(Q)) = (72, 108). In section 3 we first discard the
    75	case max{deg(P ), deg(Q)} = 120, as a straightforward application of one of our previous results
    76	in [2]. Then we discard max{deg(P ), deg(Q)} = 84 using an inequality for the intersection
    77	number proved in [6]. In section 4 we apply some transformation of K[x, y] introduced in [1]
    78	in order to reduce the size of the support of P and Q in all cases except in the discarded case
    79	(deg(P ), deg(Q)) = (80, 120). In section 5 we use the systems of polynomial equations associated
    80	to a possible counterexample as in [3] in order to discard the case (deg(P ), deg(Q)) = (66, 99) and
    81	one of the cases with (deg(P ), deg(Q)) = (72, 108). For the other case with (deg(P ), deg(Q)) =
    82	(72, 108) we couldn’t solve the corresponding system of polynomial equations, thus it is left open.
    83	   In section 6 we provide another proof of the case (deg(P ), deg(Q)) = (56, 84), without using
    84	the machinery of intersection numbers.
    85	   We want to emphasize that all the techniques developed in each of the articles [2], [3] and [5]
    86	are very useful in discarding some families of possible counterexamples. However, the Jacobian
    87	conjecture is a very hard problem, so we have to combine these techniques in order to be able
    88	to increase the lower bound of 100 for max{deg(P ), deg(Q)} established in 1983 by Moh in [10],
    89	up to 108. With enough computing power we would be able to raise it up from 108 to 125, since
    90	there is only one case left.
    91	   Since this article continues the work in [1], [2], [3] and [5], we will use the notations and
    92	conventions established in these articles.
